Your schema revealed these query types:

✔ node(id: ...)
✔ user
✔ findUser
✔ findBug
✔ allUsers
✔ allBugs

Each one works differently and gives access to different kinds of data.

Let’s break them down.

💡 1️⃣ node(id: ...)

This is the universal fetcher.

You give it a Base64 ID → it returns the actual object.

Example:

node(id: "QnVnczox") {
  ...on Bugs {
    text
  }
}


This is powerful because:

👉 ANY object type that implements Node can be fetched
👉 If the UI hides it, node() can still reveal it
👉 Flags are often hidden behind nodes

💡 2️⃣ allUsers

This returns a connection, not a list.

Meaning it comes with:

pageInfo

edges

node

So you must write:

allUsers {
  edges {
    node {
      id
      username
    }
  }
}


Use this to:

👉 enumerate all users
👉 find hidden admin accounts
👉 find suspicious usernames
👉 extract IDs for use with node()

💡 3️⃣ allBugs

Same structure as users:

allBugs {
  edges {
    node {
      id
      text
    }
  }
}


Use this to:

👉 list EVERY bug entry
👉 find hidden bugs
👉 locate suspicious text
👉 find a bug that contains the flag

💡 4️⃣ findBug(id: ...)

This lets you fetch a specific bug by number.

Example:

findBug(id: 3) {
  id
  text
}


Use this when:

👉 the bug exists but is not shown in listings
👉 you want to try IDs 1–20 to uncover hidden bugs

💡 5️⃣ findUser(id: ...)

Same idea — get a specific user.

Use this to:

👉 fetch a user found in allUsers
👉 explore fields that might hide a flag

⭐ WHY THIS IS IMPORTANT IN BUGDB v2

BugDB v2 is designed to FORCE you to use different query types to:

✔ enumerate data
✔ pivot through objects
✔ uncover hidden relationships
✔ fetch nodes the UI doesn’t show

The flag is ALWAYS in one of these nodes.

⭐ SUPER SIMPLE SUMMARY BESTIE:
👉 Yes, GraphQL has multiple query types
👉 Each one returns different shapes of data
👉 You MUST use all of them to uncover hidden objects
👉 BugDB v2 intentionally hides the flag inside one of these
